## 3 things that are fields, not folders

**Platform.** MCR covers paid social creative everywhere. TikTok does not get its own
module and neither does Snapchat, because a module is a body of knowledge, not a
placement. A Meta hook works on TikTok with pacing adjustments, so the knowledge
transfers. Use `platforms: [tiktok]`. ORG uses the same field.

**Jurisdiction.** Required on FIN. Tax and accounting rules do not travel. A card
about GST filing thresholds is useless to a US brand and dangerous if retrieved for
one. Use `jurisdiction: [IN]`, or `[global]` only for universal mechanics like the
cash conversion cycle.

**Marketplace.** Required on AMZ. Fees, gating, ad products and compliance differ by
marketplace, and Amazon India is not Amazon US. Use `marketplace: [IN]`.

**Amazon is the exception that proves the platform rule.** It gets its own module
because it is a different commerce surface, not a different placement. A Shopify
product page structure does not work as an Amazon listing. The knowledge does not
transfer, and that failure to transfer is the test.

## File naming

    MODULE_PUBLISHDATE_author-slug_topic-slug.md

- MODULE: 3 letter code of the primary module, uppercase. MULTI for cross-cutting.
- PUBLISHDATE: the date the source was PUBLISHED, not captured. `nodate` if none.
  Use `00` for an unknown day: 2026-04-00.

Examples:

    MCR_2026-03-14_dara-denney_creative-testing-structure.md
    ORG_2026-05-02_some-operator_short-form-organic-cadence.md
    FIN_2026-02-10_some-cfo_cash-conversion-cycle-dtc.md
    OPS_2026-07-04_some-operator_first-five-hires-dtc.md
    AMZ_2026-06-21_some-seller_sponsored-products-structure.md
    WLD_2025-09-18_founder-name_handwritten-notes-program.md
    MULTI_2026-01-09_ecom-masterclass_full-course.md

Publish date in the filename means sorting a folder by name gives you the library
ordered by age. In MCR, anything older than 18 months is a retirement candidate.

## The header

Every file opens with a YAML block fenced by 3 dashes. Then the body.

REQUIRED, always:

- `url`       canonical URL, or `offline`
- `rights`    owned | public_reference | licensed | study_only. NO DEFAULT.
- `why`       1 line. What is in here that a good model would not say unprompted.
              If you cannot write this line, do not capture the source.

REQUIRED sometimes:

- `date`      when the page shows none or shows the wrong one. YYYY-MM-DD or
              `unknown`. Never blank, never guessed.
- `title` `author` `org` `platform`   only when url: offline
- `mode`      on PRD: new_product | expansion | both
              on AMZ: dtc_first | amazon_first | both
- `jurisdiction`  on FIN: IN | US | UK | EU | global
- `marketplace`   on AMZ: IN | US | UK | EU | global
- `cost_ceiling`  on WLD: the bounded downside in money and time

OPTIONAL, worth using:

- `modules`   your hint, primary first, e.g. [MCR, MEA]
- `platforms` [meta, tiktok, snap, youtube, instagram, pinterest, reddit]
- `topics`    from the list in `_MODULE-GUIDE.md`
- `tier`      1 to 4, your authority read
- `scope_hint`  baseline | growth | mixed
- `watch`     point the extractor at something specific you noticed
- `covers`    timestamp ranges for long multi-topic sources
- `assets`    filenames in _assets/ that belong to this source
- `timestamps`  yes | no

NEVER write `id`. The pipeline owns source IDs.

## Tiers

    1  platform primary source. Google Search Central, Meta dev docs, Shopify docs,
       Amazon Seller Central docs. Authoritative on mechanics.
    2  large sample research or benchmark data. Baymard, academic, platform reports.
    3  named practitioner showing account-level receipts.
    4  opinion with no name or no receipts. Hypothesis fuel only.

Nothing below tier 1 may contradict tier 1 on a factual claim about how a platform
works. A practitioner can disagree with Google about what works. They cannot disagree
with Google about what the API does. A source with no date gets tier 4 regardless of
who wrote it.

## What you deliberately do not do

- Do not clean transcripts. Keep the timestamps, strip nothing else.
- Do not split long sources.
- Do not create topic subfolders until a module passes about 60 files.
- Do not create a folder per platform, jurisdiction or marketplace. Use the fields.
- Do not maintain a spreadsheet. The register is generated from the headers.
- Do not number sources.
- Do not write your own summary of the source. The `why` line is all the
  interpretation the header needs.
- Do not capture a source you cannot write a `why` for. Expect to keep 1 in 4.

## Assets

A transcript cannot see a chart. When a number is read off a dashboard, screenshot it.

    _assets/MCR_dara-denney_1422-dashboard.png

Name: MODULE_author-slug_TIMESTAMP-what-it-is.png. Then list it in `assets:`.
Without it, that segment gets held as "visual evidence required", because a model
asked to fill the gap will invent a plausible number.

## Google Docs warning

Do not type headers into a Google Doc. Docs converts straight quotes to curly quotes
and can silently break the YAML. Write plain .md or .txt files in a text editor and
upload them. If you must use Docs, turn off automatic substitution first:
Tools > Preferences > uncheck automatic substitution.

